----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[Q: b6-e3, K: e8-d7, K: e8-d8, B: f8-e7, B: g4-e6]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Evaluation:  \n- Q: b6-e3 – This moves the black queen from b6 to e3, targeting the center and potentially threatening the white queen on e2 or supporting an attack. However, e3 is somewhat exposed, and the queen may become a target for White’s pieces like the knight on f3 (if it were there) or pawns. It does increase pressure on the center but doesn’t deliver immediate threats.\n- K: e8-d7 – Moving the king one square left. This is a defensive retreat, possibly preparing to connect rooks or sidestep future checks, but it doesn’t contribute to development or attack. Kings should generally be moved cautiously in the opening/midgame unless necessary.\n- K: e8-d8 – A quiet move, possibly preparing to reroute the king or avoid potential discovered checks. Minimal strategic gain.\n- B: f8-e7 – Develops the dark-squared bishop toward the center, unpinning potentially and preparing to support kingside operations. It improves piece activity and could prepare for kingside castling (though castling isn't currently possible due to king/rook movement status). This is a solid developing move.\n- B: g4-e6 – Moves the white-squared bishop from g4 to e6, where it becomes very active—eyeing the weakened light squares around White’s king (especially f1-h3 diagonal). This could prepare tactics involving x-rays or even a threat to the white queen on e2 if supported. The bishop exerts strong pressure on key central squares.\n\nReason:  \nThe most dynamic and threatening move is **B: g4-e6**, as it places the bishop on a strong diagonal targeting White’s position, particularly pressuring the center and potentially setting up tactical ideas against the white king (e.g., if ...Qf5 or ...Ng6 follows). It maximizes activity and creates immediate discomfort for White. In contrast, the other moves are passive or only mildly improving.\n\nAnswer: B: g4-e6"}
